Lab 10: Node Isolation Using Taints and Labels

1. Apply the taint (key workload, value ivolveApp, effect NoSchedule)
bash
kubectl taint nodes worker1 workload=ivolveApp:NoSchedule

Pods will not be scheduled on this node unless they have a matching toleration.

2. Label the same node
bash
kubectl label nodes worker1 project=ivolve
3. Verify
bash
kubectl describe node worker1 | grep -i taints
# Taints:  workload=ivolveApp:NoSchedule

kubectl get nodes --show-labels
# worker1 ... project=ivolve

kubectl describe node worker1      # full view: Labels and Taints sections

#####
A pod that is allowed on this node (label will be matched with node's label and toleration will be matched with node's taint)
yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: ivolve-pod
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    project: ivolve
  tolerations:
    - key: "workload"
      operator: "Equal"
      value: "ivolveApp"
      effect: "NoSchedule"
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx
bash
kubectl apply -f ivolve-pod.yml
kubectl get pod ivolve-pod -o wide    # should run on worker1
